SWEET AKS: tell SpringBoard a passcode is set, so the lock screen demands it (2325)

ast253 set a code through Settings, the kernel stored and committed it, Settings showed "Turn Passcode Off", the
screen locked and a wake plus a swipe opened Settings with no keypad. SpringBoard made 0 keybag lock calls that boot
(selectors 8 and 32) and 0 assertions (26/27), against 11, 2 and 27 on the 0038 stub. It never learned a passcode
exists, because the one message 2321 sent on a change is delivered to a framework that does not feed it.

Why 0xd010 was not enough. Two frameworks subscribe to this service and MobileKeyBag's own aksNotificationCB
(0x1cb44e7bc, registered by MKBEventsRegister at 0x1cb44ecec) understands only two message types: 0xd00d as MKB
event 1 and 0xd00f as event 0. Every other type returns at 0x1cb44e870. SpringBoardFoundation's SBFMobileKeyBag is an
    10|MKBEvents client (-[SBFMobileKeyBag init], 0x1c16fa3fc), and the only thing outside init that refreshes its cached
has-passcode byte (+0x30) is -_queue_handleKeybagStatusChanged (0x1c16ec1c4), reached only from MKB event 1. So


That byte gates everything. -[SBFMobileKeyBag hasPasscodeSet] (0x1c16adf6c) returns it. The lock-screen keypad is
-[SBLockScreenManager lockScreenViewControllerDidPresent]'s block (0x224befa4c), which calls setPasscodeVisible: only
when it is 1. The keybag lock is -[SBFUserAuthenticationController _revokeAuthenticationImmediately:] (0x1c170c5b0),
which returns early when it is 0, so the lockSkippingGracePeriod: at 0x1c170c6e0 that is the only caller of
MKBLockDevice (0x1cb4529a0) never runs. A byte stuck at 0 is the keypad that does not appear and the 0 lock calls.

- SweetPasscode.c: a set that commits and leaves the session unlocked now raises SPC_EV_UNLOCKED as well as
  SPC_EV_PASSCODE_CHANGED, and only when the session was not already unlocked. A change on an unlocked phone is not a
    20|  lock-state change and sends only the passcode-changed message.
- SweetKeyStore.cpp: the event map runs unlocked before passcode-changed, so the byte is true before anything reads the
  change; 0xd00c is not sent at a set. The comment now names both buses, the two message types MobileKeyBag forwards,
  and the byte and call sites the diagnosis rests on.

Not changed: the stub, every selector's arguments, the two-phase stage and commit, the lock-state word (2324 already
reports ss 4 / sls 0 after a set, and the stub boot proves sls 0 is enough for SpringBoard), and the entitlement, root
and count-before-hash rules.

Host: plans/passcode/tools/check-2325.py runs springboard_test.c against the real SweetKeyStore.cpp. It models
SBFMobileKeyBag (the byte, init's selector-17 read and selector-7 bit 2), MobileKeyBag's two-type filter, the keypad
decision and the lock path, then asserts: a set makes the byte 1 and the lock call selector 8; the keypad test is
    30|true; a reboot with a code set reads it from init; turning the code off clears it; a set sends exactly unlocked then
passcode-changed; selector 7 reads 4, 4, 1, 4 across no-code, set, locked, verified. 51 assertions, 6 tests, 7
negative controls, every one caught, and 2324's 147 two-phase plus 89 selector checks unchanged.

Needs 2321, 2322, 2323, 2323b and 2324. Security code: plans/passcode/audit-brief-lock.md is the audit brief, and no
image should boot until that audit is a GO.

Check: python3 -I plans/passcode/tools/check-2325.py XNU_DIR (14 checks)
       python3 -I plans/passcode/tools/check-lock.py XNU_DIR (2321/2322)
       python3 -I plans/passcode/tools/check-2phase.py XNU_DIR (2324)
